# Motivation, ct2html and: Screw Markdown

I want to have a place where my notes and thoughts are gathered in a central place, even if they are
not really meant for public space, since I do not intend to have readers besides me and maybe some 
family member I want to send some text to.

I love @link(https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/LaTeX, `latex`), which I got introduced to in university,
and while others found `latex` to be extremely overcomplicated and used other alternative like 
@link(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typst, `typst`) or just wrote in their ugly font on their 
tablet, I defined my own math commands and had fun spamming formulas looking like sentences in 
@link(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nubian_languages, Nubean) through something like `\x`.

`latex` may be pleasing for PDF-articles, or little books, but for even smaller texts like articles,
where you would not share big compositions of settings-files for single article files.

Because of that, thinner and much "settings-less" is @link(https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markdown, 
Markdown), but it lacks what I love: Commands.

Instead of (A): Using some Markdown-dialect, or (B) another DSL for writing small articles, I came 
up with my @link(https://github.com/yungcxn/ct2html, `ct2html`) project, which takes my own DSL and 
generates @link(https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext_Markup_Language, HTML) out of it. `ct` 
(`c`an's `t`ext) is a Markdown-like language with commands and other quirks to fulfill this need.
It is a really fun opportunity to experiment with @link(https://ziglang.org/, `zig`) -- my new love 
after playing with `C` for some serious time.

Through these motivations I can mix in my profession (platform and DevOps engineering) to setup a 
bare VPS, my hobby (coding) to build a HTML-Generator and my goal (to have a space for my notes) 
alltogether.

## `ct2html`

While compilers follow usually a strict architecture of 

1. Lexing
2. Parsing
3. Semantic Analysis
4. Generation

I could skip certain steps due to reduced complexity of the language's grammar and the syntactical
"closeness" to HTML. Lexing and Parsing is merged into one step generating a flat abstract syntax
tree which holds nodes with string views to the original source text. The next step is directly 
generating the HTML according to the nodes. Semantic correctness is "kinda-checked" through the 
parsing step, as invalid syntax will not be parsed correctly into a valid AST-node.

For flexibility, a preprocessor is preset to allow importing and merging files together, which 
behaves much like the C-preprocessor. This is the overall structure:

@img: 
    ../../files/00-motivation/ct2html-architecture.png,
    100%,
    Software architecture of `ct2html` as of July 2026.

I did not have the need of really going into the fine-tuning of runtime-optimization, but having a
reasonable generation time is important for my need of dynamically generating the HTML-pages. I 
wanted to directly change files on the server and have the changes reflected immediately, so waiting 
a second or two would be bad for such a trivial algorithm.

Therefore, going through the three steps of preprocesing, parsing and generating, an abstract view
on the memory operations would look like this:

1. `Preprocessor` writes input files into a single buffer (linear)
2. `Parser` reads the buffer and generates nodes with string views to that buffer (no allocation).
   Nodes are lineraly pushed into the flat AST-array, and each new tree-layer is also linearly 
   iterated through to generate the next layer of nodes (`O(2n) = O(n)`, linear)
3. `Generator` reads nodes linearly and writes into output buffer (linear)

There might be small allocations for specific cases, like when passing an error message to the user,
but these are insignificant for the overall performance.

## Why screw Markdown (+ unknown tools)?

If there is a need for a language or a tool that would require you to learn it, while it only 
delivers a skillset over a single use-case, it is not worth the time at all. Having a 
single-digit count of hours of investment could be turned into double or triple digit hours of 
investment for a `999_999_999x` return.

An engineer of today *is in need* of projects that he can work on, and working with some other guy's
site generator tool is lazy.

Keep in mind that your punishment for using someone else's tool is that you now rely on his 
features, and in case you need something unsupported, you now either hack your way through it, 
resulting in a unsatisfactory-to-look-at solution, or you fork the project and read through the
mediocre-at-best codebase, which could eat up **a lot** of your time.

Think about me: I had a need and really no clue of compiler-construction, so I watched a video about
it, did a really bad job at first, but rewrote some architectural components and now have a working
tool for this site. I not only had fun, but could also use this to learn Zig. And another additional
effect from this project on me is that I am now really interested in compilers and want to build "a
real one". 

